\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I1}{\ignorespaces The paths show that only $\frac  {\pi }{200}$ is in the correct range to be close to the experiment quantitatively.}}{17}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {9}Figure J}{18}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {10}\textcolor {blue}{Figure K: In paper}}{18}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {K1}{\ignorespaces Shown is is the trajectory in the $xy$-plane and the evolution of the magnetic moment vector's direction (i.e. a point on the sphere in the direction of $\bm  {\mu }$).}}{19}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {11}\textcolor {red}{Figure Family D-OLD: Replaced by F}}{20}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {D1}{\ignorespaces The surface represents the $x$-value the sphere attains after $t=1 \, \text  {s}$ as a function of the initial $x$- and $y$-components of the magnetic dipole vector of the sphere. Since the $x$-direction is transverse to the rolling motion induced by gravity, it can be seen as a measure for the influence of the magnetic force on the trajectory. The graphs at the $\mu _y x$-plane are slices of the surface for fixed values of $\mu _x$, and respectively for the graphes on the $\mu _x x$-plane with fixed $\mu _y$.}}{20}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {D2}{\ignorespaces These plottings are enhanced versions of the slices of figure D1, left for different fixed $\mu _y$ and right for different fixed $\mu _x$. Keeping $\mu _y$ fixed produces symmetrical deviations from the free rolling path depending on sign and magnitude of $\mu _x$, as one would expect. A clear transition from regular sigmoid behaviour to non-smooth responses occures at highly negative values of $\mu _y$, at which the ball no longer rolls down, discussed more rigorously in D3. The slice graphs for fixed $\mu _x$ on the right show a characteristic constant curve for $\mu _x=0$, as there can be no deviation from the purely gravitational path without a torque non-parallel to the $yz$-plane. All other slices display an asymmetry for positive and negative values. This is explained by the fact that for positive $\mu _y$, the direction of rolling increases the misalignment of $\bm  {\mu }$ with $\bm  {B}$, slowing down the motion and hence also the deviation. On the other hand, negative $\mu _y$-values cause $\bm  {\mu }$ to align with $\bm  {B}$, accelerating the motion in general and hence also the deviation in particular. A less regular asymmetry occurs for larger negative $\mu _y$-values, which is again the critical region where the magnetic force dominates over and acts against gravity, causing looping paths instead of downward motion.}}{21}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {D3}{\ignorespaces Visible are different trajectories of the sphere on the $xy$-plane for selected $\mu _y$-slices and all $\mu _x$-values corresponding to these slices. Here, the anomaly in D1 and D2 for large negative $\mu _y$-values can be seen immediately. For smaller negative and all positive $\mu _y$-slices, $\mu _y$ only controls the spread of the trajectories, while $\mu _x$ influences the deviation from a straight path. At $\mu _y=-1$, the magnetic force induced by the $\mu _y$-component as soon as the $\mu _x$-induced torque bends the trajectory out of the $yz$-plane overcompensates gravity and causes an upward motion. After that, the sphere moves in roughly elliptic loops.}}{22}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {12}\textcolor {red}{Figure Family E-OLD: Replaced}}{23}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E}{\ignorespaces The graph on the left shows the $y$-projection of the sphere's COM at $t=1 \, \text  {s}$ as a function of initial $\mu _z$-values. There are roughly three discernible regions: First, the $y$-value decreases, which is expected as the rolling motion increases the misalignment of $\bm  {\mu }$ with $\bm  {B}$. On the right, the $y$-trajectory for $\mu _z=0$ is representative for this region. For increasingly high $\mu _z$-values, a transition phase occurs. The representing graph at $\mu _z = 0.95$ shows that in this region, the sphere begins to oscillate slightly at first, but gravity soon overcomes the magnetic force. For $\mu _z \geq 0.96$, the magnetic force fully overcomes the gravitational force which only causes small oscillations around the origin, as seen by the representing graph for $\mu _z=1$.}}{23}{}\protected@file@percent }
